window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][11]={"university_biology_en:M11-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M11-L1 – Neurons and signal transmission<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Animal and Human Physiology and Behaviour<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>: electrochemical\ngradients; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L5<\/a>:\nsignals.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain action potentials and\nsynaptic integration; estimate conduction velocity from latencies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A reflex connects sensory input to a motor response. The interval\nfrom stimulus to movement includes several processes, not all of which\nare electrical signal propagation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"excitable-membrane\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Excitable membrane<\/h3>\n<p>Membrane potential is the voltage inside relative to outside. Ionic\ngradients and selective conductances determine resting potential. The\nNa⁺/K⁺-ATPase maintains gradients over time, while rapid voltage changes\narise mainly from ion channels. Above threshold in an excitable membrane\nregion, regenerative opening of voltage-dependent Na⁺ channels can\ndepolarize the membrane. Na⁺ channel inactivation and delayed K⁺ channel\nopening repolarize it. No further action potential can be initiated\nduring the absolute refractory period; a stronger stimulus is needed\nduring the relative period.<\/p>\n<p>Local currents excite neighbouring membrane regions. Myelin reduces\ncapacitive loading and leakage; in myelinated axons the signal is\nregenerated predominantly at nodes of Ranvier. Suprathreshold stimulus\nintensity is often encoded by firing frequency and the population of\nactive neurons rather than proportionally larger action potentials.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"synapses-and-networks\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Synapses and networks<\/h3>\n<p>At chemical synapses, presynaptic Ca²⁺ entry can trigger vesicle\nfusion. Transmitters modify postsynaptic conductance or signalling\npathways. Excitation or inhibition depends on receptor, reversal\npotential and current membrane potential. Spatial and temporal summation\ninfluence initiation at the axon initial segment. Electrical synapses\nconnect cells through gap junctions, usually with little delay. Glial\ncells support processes including ionic balance and myelination.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_057_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Voltage trace with rest, depolarization, repolarization and afterhyperpolarization.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Voltage trace with rest, depolarization,\nrepolarization and afterhyperpolarization.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Voltage trace with rest, depolarization, repolarization\nand afterhyperpolarization.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional conduction experiment records action potentials at two\nsites of one axon with latencies of 2 and 5 ms from a common stimulus.\nSeparation is 0.12 m. Velocity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.12<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.005<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.002<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.12/(0.005-0.002)=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm/s. Subtraction cancels a common constant initiation delay. Total\nreaction time would not be appropriate here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase important despite its slower action?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Recording sites are 0.09 m apart and latency differs by 3 ms.\nCalculate velocity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the transmitter name alone not establish excitation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It maintains gradients that allow rapid channel currents.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">0.09 / 0.003 = 30 m/s.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Receptor, conductance and reversal potential determine the\neffect.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The graph is a schematic single action potential, not a measured\ntrace. Real neurons differ in channels and signal shapes; the method\nassumes matching signals at both recording sites.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Ionic currents generate electrical signals, and synapses integrate\ninformation. M11-L2 connects excitation with mechanical work.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/35-2-how-neurons-communicate\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 35.2 How Neurons Communicate<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Membrane potentials,\naction potentials and synaptic integration · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M11-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M11-L2 – Muscle and movement<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Animal and Human Physiology and Behaviour<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L1<\/a>: excitation; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4<\/a>: ATP; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L2<\/a>: levers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain sliding filaments,\nexcitation-contraction coupling and force regulation; calculate\nmechanical work.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Muscle can produce force without shortening. Mechanical work and\nenergy expenditure must therefore be distinguished.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"sarcomeres-and-cross-bridges\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sarcomeres and cross-bridges<\/h3>\n<p>Skeletal muscle fibres contain myofibrils with repeated sarcomeres\nbetween Z discs. Thin actin and thick myosin filaments slide relative to\neach other without substantially shortening themselves. During\nshortening, the I band and H zone decrease while A-band length remains\napproximately constant. Myosin heads bind actin, change conformation and\ngenerate force. ATP binding releases myosin from actin; hydrolysis\nprepares the head for the next cycle.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"coupling-and-force\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Coupling and force<\/h3>\n<p>A motor neuron releases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction.\nThe muscle action potential propagates along the membrane and T tubules.\nVoltage-dependent sensors activate Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic\nreticulum. Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, displacing the actin-binding-site\nblockade through the troponin–tropomyosin complex. Ca²⁺ pumps enable\nrelaxation. Smooth muscle instead relies mainly on Ca²⁺–calmodulin and\nmyosin light-chain regulation.<\/p>\n<p>A motor unit comprises one motor neuron and its innervated fibres.\nRecruitment and firing frequency regulate total force; force also\ndepends on sarcomere length, shortening velocity and fatigue. Isometric\naction maintains overall length; concentric action shortens active\nmuscle, while eccentric action lengthens it under tension.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_058_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two sarcomeres show greater overlap with unchanged myosin and actin filament lengths.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two sarcomeres show greater overlap with\nunchanged myosin and actin filament lengths.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two sarcomeres show greater overlap with unchanged myosin\nand actin filament lengths.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A simplified muscle exerts a constant 60 N over 0.05 m in the\ndirection of force. External work is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=Fs=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nJ. Over 0.25 s, mean power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=W/t=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nW. During isometric holding, external displacement is zero but metabolic\nenergy is still consumed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which sarcomere region stays approximately constant in length during\nshortening?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate external work for 80 N over 0.04 m in the direction of\nforce.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why cannot ATP absence simply relax cross-bridges?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The A band, because thick filament length remains constant.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">80 × 0.04 = 3.2 J.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">ATP binding is required for myosin detachment.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The sliding-filament diagram is not to scale and depicts filament\noverlap only. External work differs from chemical energy expenditure.\nThe force model ignores angles, elasticity and temporal force\nvariation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Muscle force emerges from coordinated cross-bridge activity. M11-L3\naddresses the supply of active tissues.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/38-4-muscle-contraction-and-locomotion\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 38.4 Muscle Contraction and Locomotion<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nExcitation-contraction coupling and sliding filaments · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M11-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M11-L3 – Circulation and respiration<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Animal and Human Physiology and Behaviour<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>: transport; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L3<\/a>: adaptations; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L2<\/a>: muscle.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain circulation and gas\nexchange as coupled transport processes; calculate cardiac output and\nalveolar ventilation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>High blood flow alone does not ensure high oxygen delivery.\nVentilation, diffusion, haemoglobin binding and perfusion must work\ntogether.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"circulation-and-material-transport\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Circulation and material\ntransport<\/h3>\n<p>Open circulatory systems allow haemolymph to bathe organ spaces;\nclosed systems transport blood mainly within vessels. Fish usually have\na single circuit through gills and body. Mammals have distinct pulmonary\nand systemic circuits: right heart → lungs → left heart → body → right\nheart. Valves limit backflow. Arteries carry blood away from the heart\nand veins towards it, regardless of oxygen content. Exchange occurs\npredominantly in capillaries.<\/p>\n<p>Cardiac output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=f_H V_S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelates heart rate and stroke volume. At steady state, mean ejection\nvolumes of right and left ventricles match. Flow depends on pressure\ndifference and resistance. For ideal laminar tube flow, resistance is\nproportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/r^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nreal vessels are elastic and regulated.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"respiration-and-oxygen-transport\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Respiration and oxygen\ntransport<\/h3>\n<p>Ventilation renews the medium at exchange surfaces; diffusion follows\npartial pressure gradients. Thin barriers and large areas facilitate\nexchange. Fish gills often exploit countercurrent flow, mammalian lungs\nalveolar exchange surfaces. Haemoglobin increases oxygen carrying\ncapacity; saturation depends on oxygen partial pressure, pH and\ntemperature, among other factors. Much CO₂ is transported as\nbicarbonate. Alveolar ventilation in a simple model equals breathing\nfrequency times the difference between tidal volume and physiological\ndead space. The Fick relationship connects oxygen consumption to blood\nflow and the arteriovenous oxygen content difference.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_059_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Closed circulation through right heart, lungs, left heart and body tissues.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Closed circulation through right heart,\nlungs, left heart and body tissues.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Closed circulation through right heart, lungs, left heart\nand body tissues.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Fictional steady-state model organism:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_H=75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper minute,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_S=64<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmL;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4800<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=4800<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmL/min = 4.8 L/min. At 12 breaths/min, 500 mL tidal volume and 150 mL\ndead space, alveolar ventilation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12(500-150)=4200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmL/min. With an arteriovenous content difference of 50 mL O₂/L blood,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.8\\cdot50=240<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmL O₂/min.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the pulmonary artery a vein because it carries oxygen-poor\nblood?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate Q for 80 beats/min and 75 mL/beat.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate alveolar ventilation for 15 breaths/min, 400 mL tidal\nvolume and 100 mL dead space.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No, flow away from the heart defines an artery.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">6000 mL/min = 6 L/min.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">15 × 300 = 4500 mL/min = 4.5 L/min.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>All values are illustrative, not clinical reference values. The model\nassumes steady state and constant dead space. Oxygen content and partial\npressure are different quantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Ventilation, diffusion and blood flow form a transport chain. M11-L4\naddresses water and material balances.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/19-4-cardiac-physiology\">OpenStax\n– Anatomy and Physiology 2e, 19.4 Cardiac Physiology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Cardiac\noutput and stroke volume · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/39-2-gas-exchange-across-respiratory-surfaces\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 39.2 Gas Exchange across Respiratory Surfaces<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nPartial pressure gradients and gas exchange · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M11-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M11-L4 – Osmoregulation and excretion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Animal and Human Physiology and Behaviour<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>: osmosis; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L3<\/a>: circulation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Relate osmoregulation to\nexcretion; distinguish filtration, reabsorption and secretion\nquantitatively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Freshwater fish and marine bony fish face different water and salt\nfluxes. Their metabolism must stabilize the balance even as environments\nchange.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"comparative-osmoregulation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparative osmoregulation<\/h3>\n<p>Osmoregulators maintain body fluid composition within limits partly\nindependently of their surroundings. Osmoconformers more closely match\nambient total osmolarity but can actively regulate individual ions. Many\nfreshwater fish gain water osmotically and lose salts; marine bony fish\nlose water and gain salts. Gills, gut and kidneys act together. Ammonia,\nurea and uric acid differ in water requirement, toxicity and synthesis\ncosts; excretory forms vary among species and life stages.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"nephron-and-material-balance\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nephron and material balance<\/h3>\n<p>In a mammalian nephron, glomerular filtration moves fluid into\nBowman’s capsule; blood cells and most large proteins remain in the\nvasculature. The proximal tubule reabsorbs a large proportion of\nfiltered substances. The loop of Henle contributes to the medullary\ngradient through differential water and salt permeability. Distal tubule\nand collecting duct regulate final composition. ADH increases water\npermeability of particular collecting duct cells through aquaporin-2;\naldosterone affects Na⁺ reabsorption and K⁺ secretion among other\nprocesses. Countercurrent mechanisms contribute to maintaining the\nmedullary gradient.<\/p>\n<p>For a substance in a steady-state balance model: excretion rate =\nfiltration rate − reabsorption rate + secretion rate. Filtered amount\nper time depends on glomerular filtration rate and filterable\nconcentration; it is not urine flow rate.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_060_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Arrows distinguish filtration and secretion into the lumen, reabsorption to blood and excretion.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Arrows distinguish filtration and\nsecretion into the lumen, reabsorption to blood and\nexcretion.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Arrows distinguish filtration and secretion into the\nlumen, reabsorption to blood and excretion.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional freely filtered substance has plasma concentration 2\nmmol/L and filtration rate 0.1 L/min. Filtered amount: 0.2 mmol/min. If\n0.15 mmol/min is reabsorbed and 0.02 mmol/min secreted, excretion is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.07<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2-0.15+0.02=0.07<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmmol/min. At 0.002 L/min urine flow, urine concentration is 35\nmmol/L.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In which direction does secretion occur in the tubule model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate excretion for filtration 0.4 mmol/min, reabsorption 0.3\nand secretion 0.05.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can an osmoconformer still regulate ions?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">From blood or interstitium into the tubular lumen.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">0.4 − 0.3 + 0.05 = 0.15 mmol/min.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Equal total osmolarity does not mean equal concentration of each\nion.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Values and nephron diagram are illustrative models, not diagnostic\nguidance. The balance assumes steady state and consistent substance\nunits; it does not describe individual kidney function.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Osmoregulation requires coupled membrane transport and hormonal\ncontrol. M11-L5 explores such feedback loops.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/41-2-the-kidneys-and-osmoregulatory-organs\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 41.2 The Kidneys and Osmoregulatory Organs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Nephron\nand comparative excretory organs · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/41-5-hormonal-control-of-osmoregulatory-functions\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 41.5 Hormonal Control of Osmoregulatory Functions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nHormonal regulation of water and electrolytes · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M11-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M11-L5 – Endocrinology and homeostasis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Animal and Human Physiology and Behaviour<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L5<\/a>: signalling pathways;\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L4<\/a>:\nosmoregulation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish hormone classes and\nnegative feedback; interpret a simple stability model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Homeostasis does not mean a physiological variable never changes.\nRegulation maintains functional ranges, while time of day, developmental\nstage and activity can shift the operating point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"hormonal-communication\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hormonal communication<\/h3>\n<p>Endocrine cells release signals into body fluids; paracrine signals\nact mainly locally, autocrine signals on the producing cell or similar\ncells. Peptide hormones generally act at membrane receptors; many\nsteroid and thyroid hormones regulate gene expression through\nintracellular receptors. Response time and effect depend not only on\nchemical class but also on receptors, amplification and degradation.\nHormones may act synergistically or antagonistically.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"feedback-systems\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Feedback systems<\/h3>\n<p>In the thyroid axis, hypothalamic TRH stimulates TSH release from the\nanterior pituitary; TSH promotes thyroid hormone production. Thyroid\nhormones inhibit upstream stages through feedback. In glucose\nregulation, insulin and glucagon influence tissue-specific uptake,\nstorage and mobilization of energy substrates. Negative feedback opposes\ndeviations; positive feedback amplifies changes until limited, as in\nparticular processes during childbirth.<\/p>\n<p>A simplified dynamic model describes deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom an operating point by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dx/dt=-kx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout external disturbance,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=x(0)e^{-kt}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHalf-life is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(2)/k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDelays, saturation and competing feedback loops may substantially alter\nreal behaviour.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_061_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"TRH and TSH stimulate downstream stages; thyroid hormones inhibit both upstream stages.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">TRH and TSH stimulate downstream stages;\nthyroid hormones inhibit both upstream stages.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: TRH and TSH stimulate downstream stages; thyroid hormones\ninhibit both upstream stages.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>An artificial regulation model starts with a deviation of 8 units and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmin⁻¹. After 5 min,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.94<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8e^{-1}\\approx2.94<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits remain; half-life is about 3.47 min. A larger constant accelerates\nrecovery in this model but does not establish better biological\nregulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do only appropriate target cells respond to a given\nhormone?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine half-life for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmin⁻¹.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does negative feedback affect the original disturbance?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">They possess matching receptors and downstream signalling\nmechanisms.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">ln(2)/0.1 ≈ 6.93 min.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It produces an opposing response that reduces deviation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The exponential model is a linearized assumption, not a prediction of\nhormone concentrations. Regulated quantities are dynamic; laboratory\nvalues or symptoms are not clinically interpreted here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Feedback links molecular signals to regulation of whole organs.\nM11-L6 extends the perspective to behaviour.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/37-4-regulation-of-hormone-production\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 37.4 Regulation of Hormone Production<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Endocrine\nstimuli and feedback · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/37-2-how-hormones-work\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 37.2 How Hormones Work<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Hormone receptors and\nsignalling · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M11-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M11-L6 – Behavioural biology and Tinbergen’s questions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Animal and Human Physiology and Behaviour<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>: experimental design;\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L4<\/a>: phylogeny; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L1<\/a>: neural signals.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish Tinbergen’s four\nquestions and evaluate behavioural observations with appropriate\nindependent samples.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Why does a bird sing? This may ask about neural causes, individual\nlearning experiences, consequences for reproduction or phylogenetic\norigins. None of these answers replaces the others.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"four-levels-of-explanation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Four levels of explanation<\/h3>\n<p>Tinbergen distinguishes mechanism, ontogeny, function and evolution.\nMechanism investigates current triggers and physiological processes.\nOntogeny asks how behaviour develops across an individual’s life.\nFunction examines consequences for survival and reproduction in a given\ncontext; evolution investigates the emergence and modification of the\ntrait across lineages. The first two are often called proximate\nexplanations, the last two ultimate explanations. A functional\nhypothesis cannot be established simply from the existence of a\nbehaviour.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"observation-learning-and-decision\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Observation, learning and\ndecision<\/h3>\n<p>An ethogram operationalizes observable categories such as\nvocalization, movement and rest. Focal sampling follows selected\nindividuals; scan sampling records states at predetermined times.\nObserver agreement, blinding, time of day and context matter. Multiple\nobservations of one individual are not automatically independent\nreplicates.<\/p>\n<p>Habituation is a decreasing response to repeated innocuous stimuli;\nsensory fatigue is a competing explanation. Associative learning links\nstimuli or behaviour and consequences. Inherited dispositions and\nexperience can jointly shape behavioural development. Cost-benefit\nmodels produce testable hypotheses about choices but do not assume\nconscious optimization by animals.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_062_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four complementary explanatory levels grouped into proximate and evolutionary questions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four complementary explanatory levels\ngrouped into proximate and evolutionary questions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Four complementary explanatory levels grouped into\nproximate and evolutionary questions.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Fictional ethogram: four different individuals under condition A\nspend 2, 4, 6 and 8 minutes foraging during a 20-minute observation.\nMean duration is 5 minutes, or 25% of observation time. Under condition\nB, four other individuals spend 3, 5, 7 and 9 minutes: mean 6 minutes or\n30%. The difference is 5 percentage points; without randomization and\nuncertainty analysis, a causal conclusion is unwarranted.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which of Tinbergen’s questions investigates how song changes during\nmaturation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the proportion of time spent foraging for 7 minutes out of\n20.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are ten observations of one bird not equivalent to ten\nindependent birds?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Ontogeny or individual development.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">7/20 = 35%.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Repeated observations share individual characteristics and may be\ncorrelated.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Example data are simulated; percentages are descriptive. Observation\nshould avoid disturbing animals; interventions require relevant ethical\nand legal approvals. Tinbergen’s questions complement each other and are\nnot a hierarchy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Behaviour connects mechanism, development, function and history.\nM11-L7 addresses sensory processing and temporal organization.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L7<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/45-7-behavioral-biology-proximate-and-ultimate-causes-of-behavior\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 45.7 Behavioral Biology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Behaviour, learning and\nproximate/ultimate explanations · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1439-0310.1963.tb01161.x\">Tinbergen\n(1963), On aims and methods of Ethology, 20, 410–433<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Four\nquestions; bibliographic record and historical context · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24144467/\">Tinbergen’s four\nquestions: an appreciation and an update (2013)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Abstract:\ncomplementary levels of explanation · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M11-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M11-L7 – Sensory systems and biological rhythms<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M11 – Animal and Human Physiology and Behaviour<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L1<\/a>: neural coding; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L5<\/a>: feedback; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M11&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M11-L6<\/a>: behaviour.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain sensory transduction and\nadaptation; estimate circadian periods and phases from time series.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Sensory organs detect only a subset of physical and chemical\nenvironmental stimuli. Interpretation also depends on internal state and\nbiological time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"sensory-modalities-and-coding\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sensory modalities and\ncoding<\/h3>\n<p>Mechanoreceptors detect mechanical changes, chemoreceptors chemical\nstimuli and photoreceptors light. Transduction converts stimulus energy\ninto an electrical receptor potential or altered transmitter release. A\nstronger stimulus may change firing frequency and the number of active\nsensory neurons. Receptive fields describe spatial regions where\nstimulation influences a particular sensory unit. Adaptation reduces\nsome receptors’ responses to sustained input; others continue\nresponding. Central processing compares spatial and temporal patterns.\nPerception is therefore not an unchanged copy of the sensory\nenvironment.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"biological-time\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Biological time<\/h3>\n<p>Circadian rhythms have an endogenous period of approximately 24\nhours. They may free-run under constant conditions; external time cues\nsuch as light entrain phase. Molecular feedback loops generate rhythmic\ngene expression and protein activity in many organisms. In mammals, the\nsuprachiasmatic nucleus coordinates peripheral clocks; melatonin signals\nconvey information about biological night, among other functions. Not\nevery daily fluctuation is endogenous: an immediate effect of light or\ntemperature can mimic or mask rhythmicity.<\/p>\n<p>A simple model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=M+A\\cos(2\\pi(t-\\phi)/T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\namplitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nperiod\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand phase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϕ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas time of a maximum modulo\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrequency and period are reciprocals. Reliable estimation requires\nmultiple cycles, sufficient temporal resolution and known recording\nconditions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_063_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Model curve with three maxima separated by 24.5 hours.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Model curve with three maxima separated\nby 24.5 hours.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Model curve with three maxima separated by 24.5\nhours.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional activity time series under constant conditions shows\nmaxima at 4, 28.5 and 53 hours. Two intervals of 24.5 hours give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=24.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nh. Relative to a 24-hour cue, such a free-running rhythm would drift 0.5\nh later per cycle in the absence of entrainment. Three maxima suffice\nfor demonstration but not robust uncertainty analysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does one measurement at the same time of day not establish an\nendogenous rhythm?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Estimate mean period from maxima at 3, 26 and 49 h.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What distinguishes sensory adaptation from circadian\nentrainment?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It does not distinguish endogenous oscillation from immediate\nenvironmental effects or capture periodicity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">(49 − 3)/2 = 23 h.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Adaptation changes the stimulus response; entrainment aligns the\nphase of a biological oscillator with a time cue.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The figure shows model maxima, not clinical sleep measurements. Real\nrhythms need not be sinusoidal; sampling intervals, trends and masking\ncan distort period estimates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Sensory processing and internal time connect environment with\nphysiological regulation. M12 examines how these biological systems\ndevelop.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/36-1-sensory-processes\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 36.1 Sensory Processes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sensory transduction and\nintegration · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nigms.nih.gov/education/fact-sheets/Pages/circadian-rhythms\">NIGMS\n– Circadian Rhythms<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Biological clocks, environmental entrainment\nand feedback · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
